Logic :

solved this problem using an iterative subset generation approach. 
start with the empty subset because every set always contains the empty subset. 
Then  process each element one by one.
For every existing subset, create a new subset by copying it and adding the current element. This is how new subsets are generated.
Since the input may contain duplicate elements,  first sort the array so that duplicates become adjacent. 
If the current element is not a duplicate, extend all the existing subsets.
However, if the current element is a duplicate,  extend only the subsets that were created in the previous iteration.
This prevents generating duplicate subsets while still producing every unique subset exactly once.
To achieve this efficiently,  keep track of the starting index of the subsets created in the previous iteration using the start and end variables.
Finally, after processing all elements, the result contains all unique subsets


Intuition :

A duplicate should continue only from where the previous duplicate started creating new subsets


Code :

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> outer=new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        outer.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int start=0;
        int end=0;
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            start=0;
            if(i!=0 && nums[i]==nums[i-1]){
                start=end+1;
            }
            end=outer.size()-1;
            int size=outer.size();
            for(int j=start;j<size;j++){
                List<Integer> inner=new ArrayList<>(outer.get(j));
                inner.add(nums[i]);
                outer.add(inner);
            }
        }
        return outer;
    }
}
